{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to take the requirements they infer from existing code, and the changes they propose to existing requirement text, through the review and agreement of {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill` before committing them or tagging code against them. It MUST state that a general request to bootstrap is not agreement to specific text, and MUST direct agents to keep requirements the user rejects or defers on the gap list of {cite}`requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill`. Each area's agreed requirements MUST be committed together before that area's code is tagged.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The text of `requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill.review` states only what the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must direct agents to do. It prescribes no library, algorithm, signature, type or field. The one structural-looking clause is the delegation to the review and agreement of `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill`. The root `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md` meets it. Workflow step 5 says to \"Review, agree, and commit them through habeas-requirements steps 3 to 5\", which names the habeas-requirements skill. Step 1 and the Proposing and Tagging sections point to that same skill.\n\nI checked each clause against the root:\n- **Inferred requirements and proposed text changes go through review.** Step 4 has agents propose requirements and \"any changes to their text\" as uncommitted edits. Step 5 sends them through the habeas-requirements review, agreement and commit steps. Step 5 also carries a gate: \"don't commit a requirement or tag code against it until the user agrees to its text\".\n- **A general request to bootstrap is not agreement.** Step 5 states this in those words: \"a general request to bootstrap is not agreement to specific text\".\n- **Rejected or deferred requirements stay on the gap list.** Step 5 says to remove drafts the user rejects, defers or has not decided from the chapter, and to \"keep them on the gap list\". The Inventories section defines the gap list, and the Report section lists it.\n- **Commit before tagging.** Step 5 (review, agree, commit) comes before step 6 (tag the area's tests and root items). The Tagging section only tags requirements that have been agreed, and step 4 keeps each area's requirements in one chapter file.\n\nI found no structural obligation that needs {impl} evidence beyond the skill file. A skill that restated the review inline instead of pointing to the habeas-requirements skill would still direct the same behavior, so the delegation is discretionary rather than structural. Test adequacy is outside this assessment.\n\nbehavioral: The test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_skill_reviews_inferred_requirements_before_committing_or_tagging]` carries a {test} reference and covers each obligation in `requirements.cli.bootstrap_skill.review`. It checks the bundled `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` SKILL.md text. That is the observable behavior, since the requirement is about what the skill directs.\n\n- **Review and agreement before commit or tagging, for inferred requirements.**\n  - The test asserts the phrase \"every requirement starts as a proposal\".\n  - It finds the step numbers for \"Propose requirements for the area\", for \"Review, agree, and commit them through habeas-requirements steps 3 to 5\", and for \"Tag the area's tests and root items\". It asserts propose < review < tag.\n  - It asserts that the review step contains the gate \"don't commit a requirement or tag code against it until the user agrees to its text\".\n- **Proposed changes to existing requirement text.** The test asserts that the propose step contains \"proposing any changes to their text\".\n- **A general request to bootstrap is not agreement.** The test asserts the review step contains \"a general request to bootstrap is not agreement to specific text\".\n- **Rejected or deferred requirements stay on the gap list.** The test asserts the review step contains \"Remove drafts the user rejects, defers, or has not decided from the chapter, and keep them on the gap list\".\n- **Each area's requirements are committed together before that area's code is tagged.**\n  - The commit happens in the review step, which the test shows comes before the tag step.\n  - The test asserts that the Workflow section contains \"Repeat steps {propose} to 7 for each area\". That makes the per-area cycle cover both commit and tag.\n  - It asserts that `habeas-requirements` contains \"commit it, all groups together\". That is the commit behavior the bootstrap skill's review step delegates to.\n\nThe helpers (`step`, `step_of`, `assert_text`, `assert_phrases`, `section`) are reached from the marked test and perform real `contains` assertions. The SKILL.md workflow paragraph for step 5 contains the asserted wording. I found no unmarked relevant test and no uncovered obligation.",
  "references": "53d07a5a10841f825e237cc58ee3255021ac30b43b855eeb41901fd95818f143",
